WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.030 align:start position:0%
 
rape as a weapon  has the world failed to protect women  our guest today is monica hauser founder  of the organization medical mondiale  which helps traumatized women and girls  who live in war zones and conflict  regions  hello hello  welcome to the dw interview  i'm alexandra fornamen  this topic has been in the news recently  with the brutal attacks by islamic state  forces in syria and iraq  thousands of women many of them yazidis  have been kidnapped raped and sold into  slavery  why do men do this  there's a huge problem right now with  sexual violence around the world  but only some incidents make the  headlines  there's also a lot of violence in  so-called peaceful states  where people live in patriarchal  societies  of course in conflict zones sexual  violence can be used strategically to  achieve certain goals  to demoralize entire populations to  humiliate degrade or intimidate them  but women are raped in these situations  simply because the men have weapons  and they can do whatever they want  that's a pretty somber evaluation you've  been dealing with this problem for more  than 20 years are you surprised that  these things are still happening  i'm not surprised that they do  we've been dealing with these situations  for 22 years  other activists have been working a lot  longer than that to stop violence  against women  i know what the reality is  i read reports mostly from syria but  also from the democratic republic of  congo and other conflict regions  the details in these reports are simply  appalling  for example  i become extremely upset when i read  about the rape of young girls  it's a clear indication of how brutal  these men can be  they dehumanize their victims because  they themselves have become dehumanized  have your experiences and research in  this field changed your image of men in  general  i had always had a very critical image  of men so for me that's nothing  at the same time i think that people are  not born brutal or violent  their behavior is dictated by  circumstances  i believe that a lot has gone wrong in  this world in the last 20 or 30 years  especially as a result of the west's  reaction to the 9 11 attacks  a lot of young men throughout the arab  world have been treated brutally and  they have no hope for the future  still it is the patriarchal systems  worldwide that create opportunities for  men to behave like this  your primary goal is to help women  who've been subjected to violence you've  launched a new project in northern iraq  tell us about that  we provide support for women and girls  who are traumatized by their experiences  this includes direct psychological  medical and legal support  we also help local caregivers to develop  new skills  we're doing that right now in the doha  region of northern iraq in cooperation  with the kurdish government there  these local caregivers are often the  first people that these women turn to  many of the victims are yasidis the  caregivers include doctors nurses and  psychologists and these people need  training so that they can deal properly  with the victims  they have to learn how to provide  support and they must do it with the  utmost care  and not make things worse  so most of the caregivers don't have any  experience dealing with these kinds of  victims these  qualifications  very few people do i'm sorry to say that  even in western countries like germany  many of those who deal with refugee  women don't have this kind of training  that situation has got to  it's also important to train local  caregivers in conflict zones because  entire societies have been traumatized  and will stay that way for a long time  if we can provide trained personnel to  help them  it will have a positive effect over time  you've been working with female victims  of violence for more than 20 years in  1992 you heard about incidents of mass  rape during the bosnian war and decided  to go there to help  that was a radical step  what was your primary motivation  but when i read these reports i realized  that i had to do something  i was angry about what was being done to  women there and angry that the  international community and the aid  organizations were doing nothing to help  them  and i was angry about the neurid reports  that i saw in the media  i felt that this situation affected me  personally and that i had to go to  bosnia and do what i could to help those  women  and once you got there was there a  particular person or event that had a  real impact on you  i remember the entire year of 1992-93  quite well  i sometimes felt like a war veteran  because i realized that i too had been  traumatized by living and working in a  war zone  i simply cannot forget what i saw and  experienced there  i still have vivid memories of meeting  nurses who worked at a hospital in  zenika in central bosnia  they really wanted to work with me many  of the patients were bosnian muslim  women who'd managed to escape the  fighting  some were pregnant and wanted an  abortion  but the chief physician wouldn't allow  it  the nurses talked to these women and  learned that many others had committed  suicide  the nurses decided that these women  needed a special facility where they  could feel safe  and get the psychological and medical  support that they needed  you mentioned that you also felt  traumatized at this time how did you  deal with your situation  were you able to put those horrific  stories out of your mind at the end of  the day  no i didn't want to become cynical or  just go through the motions  i've seen that happen to a lot of other  people in this profession  that means that when you've come through  a particularly difficult situation you  can't keep looking back at what happened  you'll feel like you're on a treadmill  you have to deal with it bit by bit  and quite often your response is based  on your own experience and your own  feeling of powerlessness  so it's important for those of us who do  this kind of work every day to pause  occasionally and reflect  what role do we play and how can we  protect ourselves  but it's also important for us to  understand why we do this work  much of my motivation comes from the  fact that my colleagues and i want to  make the world a better place for women  i've met women all around the world in  liberia congo bosnia afghanistan  and we talk about these successes and  failures in our work  we're doing this not only for ourselves  but also for our daughters to change  things in this world you've got to stick  together and we believe strongly that  every time we help a woman in need it  makes the world a better place  so that situations such as in northern  iraq where women are raped abducted and  enslaved no longer happen  it's a very sad situation and i am  disappointed by the response of the  international community  for 22 years we've been reporting on the  fate of women in war zones  right now we're working on a report that  indicates that many bosnian women are  still suffering from psychological  trauma 22 years after the fact  they are outcasts in their own society  so it's a double trauma if you will  the un has drafted proposals and germany  has signed them  but no one appears willing to stand up  and take action to change the situation  some people have come up with good ideas  but where does the money end up  what are the priorities in this society  and as far as weapons exports are  concerned germany for example is selling  weapons to saudi arabia and other  countries in conflict zones  and that won't help improve the  situation of women there  and in these unstable conflict zones  there's an increase in the number of  rapes and that just makes the situation  worse  what specific recommendations would you  make  there's got to be substantial changes in  foreign security and defense policy  we have to take measures to prevent  conflict and that means not selling  weapons to ukraine or saudi arabia  what we need  is more diplomacy and the situation of  women in conflict zones must be  monitored more closely  also women should be given a role in  peace negotiations and it's tragic that  women haven't been given more of a say  in how refugee camps are run  for example there have been incidents of  sexual slavery in syria and adjacent  countries  that wouldn't happen if women were  allowed to help administer the camps  we've known about these problems for  years but no one does anything about  them  there have been lots of fine proposals  but people don't have the political will  to carry them out and i'm still waiting  for the german government to do more to  help women in conflict zones  einstein  finally we ask our interview guests to  complete a series of three sentences  first  when my son talks about my work he says  he loves it and hates it when i go to  afghanistan he hates it because it's  dangerous for me but he also loves it  because he knows it's my job  chancellor merkel were to call me today  and offered to grant me one wish i'd say  i'd like to take her to see our work in  conflict zones if she could see that  maybe she'd change her policy priorities  in the politics  my colleagues and i would dissolve our  women's aid organization  we won't see that in our lifetime  we'll stop our work only when there is  no more sexual violence against women  that's our vision  we keep working even though we know that  we won't reach our goal  monica thank you my pleasure

